{:edb/db-before-t 1690 :edb/db-after-t 1691 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535003 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:01:37.601Z" 13194139535003 true] [17592186220659 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220661 13194139535003 true] [17592186220664 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220666 13194139535003 true] [17592186220668 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220671 13194139535003 true] [17592186221094 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2+3xy, \\qquad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 1.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new},$ in terms of the new value $x_\\text{new}$ and the current value $y.$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186221094 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x^2+3xy, \\qquad y(0)=-1$\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 1$.\nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new}$, in terms of the new value $x_\\text{new}$ and the current value $y$." 13194139535003 false] [17592186221094 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} + 3x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - 3x_{new}y_{new} &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} \\\\\ny_{new}(1 - 3x_{new}) &= y + 2x_{new}^{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + 2x_{new}^{2}}{1 - 3x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535003 false] [17592186221094 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186221095 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2y + e^x, \\qquad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right).$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186221095 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x^2y + e^x, \\qquad y(0)=-1$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$." 13194139535003 false] [17592186221095 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(x_{new}^{2}y_{new} + e^{x_{new}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}y_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}} \\\\\n(1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2})y_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{y + \\frac{1}{2}e^{x_{new}}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}x_{new}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (-1 + \\frac{1}{2}e^{1/2})/(1 - \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(\\sqrt{e} - 2))/(7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right)\n$$" 13194139535003 false] [17592186221095 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186221113 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x^2 - y, \\qquad y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186221113 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 4x^2 - y, \\qquad y(0)=0$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1)$." 13194139535003 false] [17592186221113 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1)$. The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (4(x_{new})^{2} - y_{new}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2(x_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2(x_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{2}y_{new} &= y + 2(x_{new})^{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nand $y(1)$.\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= (2(0 + 2(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= (2(y + 2(x_{new})^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= (2(\\frac{1}{3} + 2 \\cdot 1^{2}))/(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad y(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}\n$$" 13194139535003 false] [1759218622111