lue problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-4x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(2)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326027 1113 #uuid "f75dcc74-76ed-50af-9566-63f6f6a83994" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326027 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+2=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-8\\cdot 2}{1-2\\cdot {2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{7} \\\\  & ≈2.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈2.14.$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326029 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326030 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326031 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326032 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1041 "field-1" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326029 13194139535003 true] [17592186326028 1110 #uuid "d6a75e46-943f-5a9b-9e6d-4d9b80905b8b" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326029 1034 "2.14" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535003 true] [17592186326029 1111 #uuid "d50a28f9-cb8f-56b3-a1f9-1056ca545f34" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326030 1034 "0.19" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535003 true] [17592186326030 1111 #uuid "a6dfc6d1-904a-5609-b822-4e79c4c46b38" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326031 1034 "0.01" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535003 true] [17592186326031 1111 #uuid "a9482fcf-7fdd-535d-be01-fa12386d052b" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326032 1034 "1.71" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535003 true] [17592186326032 1111 #uuid "2d3833d5-3283-5935-a734-f45b4ae49193" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326034 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1089 "q-339695" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{e}^{x}+1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1113 #uuid "3dd9c8e3-0a5f-5518-8268-fe808abca826" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326033 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e} \\\\  & ≈-0.18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.18.$" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326035 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326036 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326037 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326038 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1041 "field-1" 13194139535003 true] [17592186326034 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326038 13194139535003 true] [17592186